{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Return a list of prime factors of a given integer in ascending order.\n    Each factor is included the exact number of times it appears in the factorization.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    factors = []\n    divisor = 2\n\n    # Trial division algorithm for finding prime factors\n    while divisor * divisor <= n:\n        while n % divisor == 0:\n            factors.append(divisor)\n            n //= divisor\n        divisor += 1\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Return list of prime factors of given integer in the order from smallest to largest.\n    Each of the factors should be listed number of times corresponding to how many times it appeares in factorization.\n    Input number should be equal to the product of all factors\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    factors = []\n\n    # Handle special case for 1, which has no prime factors\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    d = 2\n    temp_n = n  # Use a temporary variable for the factorization process\n\n    # Continuously divide by d until n is no longer divisible\n    while d * d <= temp_n:\n        while temp_n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            temp_n //= d\n        d += 1  # Increment d to check the next potential prime factor\n\n    # If temp_n is still greater than 1 after the loop, it means temp_n itself is a prime factor\n    if temp_n > 1:\n        factors.append(temp_n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
