{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "Here's the implementation of the `find_zero` function:\n\n```python\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    # Newton-Raphson method implementation\n    x = 0.0  # Starting point\n    tolerance = 1e-10\n    max_iterations = 1000\n\n    for i in range(max_iterations):\n        # Calculate f(x) using the poly function\n        f_x = poly(xs, x)\n\n        # Calculate derivative f'(x)\n        # For polynomial with coefficients [a0, a1, a2, ..., an]\n        # derivative has coefficients [a1, 2*a2, 3*a3, ..., n*an]\n        derivative_coeffs = [i * xs[i] for i in range(1, len(xs))]\n        f_prime_x = poly(derivative_coeffs, x) if derivative_coeffs else 0\n\n        # Check for convergence\n        if abs(f_x) < tolerance:\n            return x\n\n        # Avoid division by zero\n        if abs(f_prime_x) < 1e-15:\n            break\n\n        # Newton-Raphson formula: x_new = x - f(x)/f'(x)\n        x_new = x - f_x / f_prime_x\n\n        # Check for convergence by change in x\n        if abs(x_new - x) < tolerance:\n            return x_new\n\n        x = x_new\n\n    return x\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Extract values and their original indices that are divisible by three.\n    divisible_by_three_values = []\n    divisible_by_three_indices = []\n    for i, val in enumerate(l):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            divisible_by_three_values.append(val)\n            divisible_by_three_indices.append(i)\n\n    # Sort the extracted values.\n    sorted_extracted_values = sorted(divisible_by_three_values)\n\n    # Create a new list as a shallow copy of the original.\n    result_list = list(l)\n\n    # Place the sorted values back into the new list at their original divisible-by-three indices.\n    for i, original_index in enumerate(divisible_by_three_indices):\n        result_list[original_index] = sorted_extracted_values[i]\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    # Extract elements at indices divisible by 3 (0, 3, 6, ...)\n    elements_at_divisible_indices = []\n    for i in range(0, len(l), 3):\n        elements_at_divisible_indices.append(l[i])\n\n    # Sort the extracted elements\n    sorted_elements = sorted(elements_at_divisible_indices)\n\n    # Create result list by copying original list\n    result = l[:]\n\n    # Place sorted elements back at indices divisible by 3\n    sorted_index = 0\n    for i in range(0, len(l), 3):\n        result[i] = sorted_elements[sorted_index]\n        sorted_index += 1\n\n    return result\n```"}
