{"task_id": "HumanEval/121", "solution": "```python\ndef solution(lst):\n    \"\"\"Given a non-empty list of integers, return the sum of all of the odd elements that are in even positions.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Helper function to check if a value is odd.\n    def _is_odd(value: int) -> bool:\n        return value % 2 != 0\n\n    # Helper function to check if an index is even.\n    def _is_even_position(index: int) -> bool:\n        return index % 2 == 0\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    for index, element in enumerate(lst):\n        if _is_even_position(index) and _is_odd(element):\n            total_sum += element\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/120", "solution": "```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef maximum(arr, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array arr of integers and a positive integer k, return a sorted list\n    of length k with the maximum k numbers in arr.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle edge cases\n    if k == 0:\n        return []\n\n    if k >= len(arr):\n        return sorted(arr)\n\n    # Use a min-heap to efficiently find k largest elements\n    # For small k relative to array size, heap is most efficient\n    if k <= len(arr) // 2:\n        min_heap = []\n        for num in arr:\n            if len(min_heap) < k:\n                heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)\n            elif num > min_heap[0]:\n                heapq.heapreplace(min_heap, num)\n        return sorted(min_heap)\n    else:\n        # For large k, it's more efficient to sort and slice\n        return sorted(arr)[-k:]\n```"}
