{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "def get_odd_collatz(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return a sorted list that has the odd numbers in collatz sequence.\n\n    The Collatz conjecture is a conjecture in mathematics that concerns a sequence defined\n    as follows: start with any positive integer n. Then each term is obtained from the\n    previous term as follows: if the previous term is even, the next term is one half of\n    the previous term. If the previous term is odd, the next term is 3 times the previous\n    term plus 1. The conjecture is that no matter what value of n, the sequence will always reach 1.\n\n    Note:\n        1. Collatz(1) is [1].\n        2. returned list sorted in increasing order.\n\n    For example:\n    get_odd_collatz(5) returns [1, 5] # The collatz sequence for 5 is [5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1], so the odd numbers are only 1, and 5.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle the special case for n=1\n    if n == 1:\n        return [1]\n\n    # Generate the Collatz sequence\n    sequence = []\n    current = n\n    while current != 1:\n        sequence.append(current)\n        if current % 2 == 0:\n            current = current // 2\n        else:\n            current = 3 * current + 1\n    sequence.append(1)  # Add the final 1\n\n    # Extract odd numbers from the sequence\n    odd_numbers = [num for num in sequence if num % 2 != 0]\n\n    # Return sorted list in ascending order\n    return sorted(odd_numbers)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "Looking at the generated code, I need to provide a cleaner, more direct implementation that follows the exact function signature from the original problem. Let me extract just the function without the class wrapper:\n\n```python\ndef get_odd_collatz(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return a sorted list that has the odd numbers in collatz sequence.\n\n    The Collatz conjecture is a conjecture in mathematics that concerns a sequence defined\n    as follows: start with any positive integer n. Then each term is obtained from the\n    previous term as follows: if the previous term is even, the next term is one half of\n    the previous term. If the previous term is odd, the next term is 3 times the previous\n    term plus 1. The conjecture is that no matter what value of n, the sequence will always reach 1.\n\n    Note:\n        1. Collatz(1) is [1].\n        2. returned list sorted in increasing order.\n\n    For example:\n    get_odd_collatz(5) returns [1, 5] # The collatz sequence for 5 is [5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1], so the odd numbers are only 1, and 5.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Generate the complete Collatz sequence\n    sequence = []\n    current = n\n\n    while True:\n        sequence.append(current)\n        if current == 1:\n            break\n        # Apply Collatz rule: even -> n/2, odd -> 3n+1\n        if current % 2 == 0:\n            current = current // 2\n        else:\n            current = 3 * current + 1\n\n    # Extract odd numbers from the sequence\n    odd_numbers = [num for num in sequence if num % 2 != 0]\n\n    # Return sorted list in ascending order\n    return sorted(odd_numbers)\n```"}
