{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "Here's the implementation of the Tribonacci sequence function:\n\n```python\nclass MemoizationCache:\n    \"\"\"Manages cached computation results for the Tribonacci sequence.\n    Stores and retrieves values by index.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._cache = {}\n\n    def store(self, index: int, value: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Stores a computed value in the cache.\"\"\"\n        self._cache[index] = value\n\n    def retrieve(self, index: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Retrieves a cached value.\"\"\"\n        return self._cache[index]\n\n    def exists(self, index: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a value for the given index exists in the cache.\"\"\"\n        return index in self._cache\n\n    def clear(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Clears all contents from the cache.\"\"\"\n        self._cache.clear()\n\n\ndef tri(n):\n    \"\"\"Computes the first n+1 numbers of the modified Tribonacci sequence.\n    Handles forward references in recursive definitions using memoization.\"\"\"\n\n    cache = MemoizationCache()\n\n    def _compute_single_value(index_val: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes a single Tribonacci value, utilizing and updating the memoization cache.\n        Handles base cases, even, and odd index formulas, resolving forward references.\"\"\"\n\n        if cache.exists(index_val):\n            return cache.retrieve(index_val)\n\n        if index_val == 0:\n            value = 1\n        elif index_val == 1:\n            value = 3\n        elif index_val % 2 == 0:  # Even n > 1\n            value = 1 + index_val // 2\n        else:  # Odd n > 1\n            term1 = _compute_single_value(index_val - 1)\n            term2 = _compute_single_value(index_val - 2)\n            term3 = _compute_single_value(index_val + 1)\n            value = term1 + term2 + term3\n\n        cache.store(index_val, value)\n        return value\n\n    results = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        results[i] = _compute_single_value(i)\n\n    return results\n```\n\nThis implementation handles the complex recursive definition of the Tribonacci sequence by using memoization to resolve forward references efficiently. The function correctly implements all the given rules and returns the first n+1 numbers of the sequence as required."}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Create a function that takes a string as input which contains only square brackets.\n    The function should return True if and only if there is a valid subsequence of brackets\n    where at least one bracket in the subsequence is nested.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Find all maximal valid contiguous bracket subsequences\n    def find_valid_subsequences(s):\n        balance = 0\n        start_index = 0\n        valid_sequences = []\n\n        for i, char in enumerate(s):\n            if char == '[':\n                balance += 1\n            elif char == ']':\n                balance -= 1\n\n            if balance < 0:\n                # Malformed sequence, reset and start new potential sequence\n                balance = 0\n                start_index = i + 1\n            elif balance == 0:\n                # Found a maximal valid contiguous sequence\n                if start_index <= i:\n                    valid_sequences.append(s[start_index:i + 1])\n                start_index = i + 1\n\n        return valid_sequences\n\n    # Check if a sequence has nested structure\n    def has_nested_structure(sequence):\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth = 0\n\n        for char in sequence:\n            if char == '[':\n                current_depth += 1\n                max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n            elif char == ']':\n                current_depth -= 1\n\n        return max_depth >= 2\n\n    # Main logic\n    valid_subsequences = find_valid_subsequences(string)\n\n    for seq in valid_subsequences:\n        if has_nested_structure(seq):\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "def is_nested(string):\n    '''\n    Checks if a string containing only square brackets has at least one valid and nested bracket pair.\n    Returns True for nested patterns like `[[]]`, False otherwise.\n    '''\n\n    def _contains_only_brackets(s):\n        '''\n        Helper to check if the string contains only '[' and ']'.\n        Returns False for empty strings or if any invalid characters are found.\n        '''\n        if not s:\n            return False\n        for char in s:\n            if char not in ['[', ']']:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def _has_nesting_detector(s):\n        '''\n        Helper to detect nesting in a bracket string with O(n) time and O(1) space.\n        It identifies `[[...]]` patterns only if no unmatched closing brackets precede them,\n        which aligns with \"valid subsequence\".\n        '''\n        current_depth = 0\n        # Flag to track if any ']' was encountered without a preceding open '['.\n        # Such unmatched ']' characters indicate a break in forming a 'valid subsequence'\n        # for brackets prior to this point.\n        encountered_unmatched_closing = False\n\n        for char in s:\n            if char == '[':\n                current_depth += 1\n            elif char == ']':\n                if current_depth > 0:\n                    # This ']' matches an open '['. Check for nesting.\n                    # A nested pair is found if current depth is 2 or more\n                    # and no unmatched closing brackets were seen before this nested pair.\n                    if current_depth >= 2 and not encountered_unmatched_closing:\n                        return True\n                    current_depth -= 1\n                else:\n                    # This ']' is unmatched as current_depth is 0.\n                    # Mark that such an event has occurred.\n                    encountered_unmatched_closing = True\n        return False\n\n    # Input Validation Layer: Ensures the string is not empty and contains only brackets.\n    if not _contains_only_brackets(string):\n        return False\n\n    # Nesting Detection Layer: Performs the core nesting check.\n    return _has_nesting_detector(string)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "```python\ndef minPath(grid, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the lexicographically smallest path of exactly k cells in an N\u00d7N grid.\n    Movement is allowed to adjacent cells sharing edges, and cells can be revisited.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    N = len(grid)\n\n    # Data Layer Components\n    class Position:\n        \"\"\"Represents a coordinate in the grid.\"\"\"\n        __slots__ = ['row', 'col']\n\n        def __init__(self, row, col):\n            self.row = row\n            self.col = col\n\n        def __eq__(self, other):\n            if not isinstance(other, Position):\n                return NotImplemented\n            return self.row == other.row and self.col == other.col\n\n        def __hash__(self):\n            return hash((self.row, self.col))\n\n    class Grid:\n        \"\"\"Manages the N\u00d7N grid data and provides lookup utilities.\"\"\"\n        __slots__ = ['_cells', '_size', '_value_positions']\n\n        def __init__(self, cells_data):\n            self._cells = cells_data\n            self._size = len(cells_data)\n            self._value_positions = {}\n            for r in range(self._size):\n                for c in range(self._size):\n                    self._value_positions[self._cells[r][c]] = Position(r, c)\n\n        def get_neighbors(self, pos):\n            \"\"\"Returns a list of valid adjacent positions.\"\"\"\n            neighbors = []\n            deltas = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]  # Up, Down, Left, Right\n            for dr, dc in deltas:\n                new_row, new_col = pos.row + dr, pos.col + dc\n                if 0 <= new_row < self._size and 0 <= new_col < self._size:\n                    neighbors.append(Position(new_row, new_col))\n            return neighbors\n\n        def get_value(self, pos):\n            \"\"\"Returns the value at a given position.\"\"\"\n            return self._cells[pos.row][pos.col]\n\n    class Path:\n        \"\"\"Manages the current path state during DFS traversal.\"\"\"\n        __slots__ = ['values', 'positions']\n\n        def __init__(self):\n            self.values = []\n            self.positions = []\n\n        def add_value(self, value, position):\n            \"\"\"Appends a value and its position to the path.\"\"\"\n            self.values.append(value)\n            self.positions.append(position)\n\n        def remove_last_value(self):\n            \"\"\"Removes the last added value and position (backtracking).\"\"\"\n            self.values.pop()\n            self.positions.pop()\n\n        def is_complete(self, target_length):\n            \"\"\"Checks if the path has reached the target length.\"\"\"\n            return len(self.values) == target_length\n\n        def get_values(self):\n            \"\"\"Returns the list of values in the path.\"\"\"\n            return self.values\n\n    # Algorithm Layer Components\n    class PathExplorer:\n        \"\"\"Implements DFS traversal with backtracking and pruning.\"\"\"\n        __slots__ = ['_grid', '_target_length', '_current_best_path_ref']\n\n        def __init__(self, grid_instance, target_length, current_best_path_ref):\n            self._grid = grid_instance\n            self._target_length = target_length\n            self._current_best_path_ref = current_best_path_ref # Reference to PathFinder's best_path\n\n        def _is_lexicographically_smaller(self, path_a_values, path_b_values):\n            \"\"\"Compares two paths lexicographically, assuming equal length.\"\"\"\n            for i in range(self._target_length):\n                if path_a_values[i] < path_b_values[i]:\n                    return True\n                if path_a_values[i] > path_b_values[i]:\n                    return False\n            return False # Paths are equal\n\n        def dfs_explore(self, current_pos, current_path, visited_positions_param_unused):\n            \"\"\"Recursively explores paths starting from current_pos with pruning.\"\"\"\n            current_value = self._grid.get_value(current_pos)\n            current_path.add_value(current_value, current_pos)\n\n            # Pruning: If a complete best path has been found, and the current path's prefix\n            # is already lexicographically greater, then prune this branch.\n            if self._current_best_path_ref[0] is not None:\n                current_len = len(current_path.values)\n                # Only compare if the current path's length is relevant for prefix comparison\n                if current_len <= self._target_length:\n                    for i in range(current_len):\n                        current_val = current_path.values[i]\n                        best_val_at_i = self._current_best_path_ref[0][i]\n\n                        if current_val > best_val_at_i:\n                            current_path.remove_last_value() # Backtrack\n                            return # Prune this branch\n                        elif current_val < best_val_at_i:\n                            break # Current path is better so far, continue exploring\n\n            if current_path.is_complete(self._target_length):\n                # If a complete path is found, compare and update the global best.\n                if self._current_best_path_ref[0] is None or \\\n                   self._is_lexicographically_smaller(current_path.get_values(), self._current_best_path_ref[0]):\n                    self._current_best_path_ref[0] = list(current_path.get_values()) # Update with a copy\n            else:\n                # Explore neighbors if the path is not yet complete.\n                neighbors = self._grid.get_neighbors(current_pos)\n                for next_pos in neighbors:\n                    # The 'visited_positions_param_unused' is passed to adhere to design signature, but not used.\n                    # Paths can revisit cells (\"not necessarily distinct\").\n                    self.dfs_explore(next_pos, current_path, visited_positions_param_unused)\n\n            current_path.remove_last_value() # Backtrack\n\n    class PathFinder:\n        \"\"\"Coordinates the path finding process by iterating starting points.\"\"\"\n        __slots__ = ['_grid', '_path_length', 'best_path_storage']\n\n        def __init__(self, grid_data, path_len):\n            self._grid = Grid(grid_data)\n            self._path_length = path_len\n            # Use a list to hold the best path, allowing PathExplorer to update it by reference.\n            self.best_path_storage = [None]\n\n        def find_minimum_path(self):\n            \"\"\"Initiates the search for the minimum path from all starting positions.\"\"\"\n\n            # Special case for k=1: The minimum path is just the smallest value in the grid.\n            if self._path_length == 1:\n                min_val = float('inf')\n                for r in range(self._grid._size):\n                    for c in range(self._grid._size):\n                        min_val = min(min_val, self._grid._cells[r][c])\n                return [min_val]\n\n            # Iterate through all possible starting positions in the grid.\n            for r in range(self._grid._size):\n                for c in range(self._grid._size):\n                    start_pos = Position(r, c)\n                    explorer = PathExplorer(self._grid, self._path_length, self.best_path_storage)\n                    current_path = Path()\n                    # An empty set is passed for 'visited_positions' parameter as it's not used.\n                    explorer.dfs_explore(start_pos, current_path, set())\n\n            return self.best_path_storage[0]\n\n    # Main execution for the minPath function\n    path_finder_instance = PathFinder(grid, k)\n    result = path_finder_instance.find_minimum_path()\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/133", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef sum_squares(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Takes a list of numbers, rounds each to upper integer (ceiling),\n    squares each rounded number, and returns sum of all squared numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    for num in lst:\n        ceiling_value = math.ceil(num)\n        squared_value = ceiling_value ** 2\n        total += squared_value\n    return total\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/134", "solution": "```python\ndef check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(txt):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the last character of a string is an alphabetical, standalone letter.\n    Returns True if standalone and alphabetical, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _is_alphabetical(char):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a character is an uppercase or lowercase letter.\"\"\"\n        return 'a' <= char.lower() <= 'z'\n\n    def _has_trailing_spaces(s):\n        \"\"\"Determines if the string ends with one or more spaces.\"\"\"\n        return len(s) > 0 and s[-1] == ' '\n\n    def _is_part_of_word(s, position):\n        \"\"\"Checks if the character at 'position' is part of a word based on its preceding character.\"\"\"\n        # For the last character, it's part of a word if the character before it is alphabetical.\n        if position == 0:\n            return False # A single character at the start cannot be part of a preceding word.\n        return _is_alphabetical(s[position - 1])\n\n\n    # Handle empty string as an edge case.\n    if not txt:\n        return False\n\n    # Handle strings ending with trailing spaces.\n    if _has_trailing_spaces(txt):\n        return False\n\n    last_char = txt[-1]\n    last_char_position = len(txt) - 1\n\n    # Ensure the last character is alphabetical.\n    if not _is_alphabetical(last_char):\n        return False\n\n    # Ensure the last character is not part of a word.\n    if _is_part_of_word(txt, last_char_position):\n        return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/133", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef sum_squares(lst):\n    \"\"\"You are given a list of numbers.\n    You need to return the sum of squared numbers in the given list,\n    round each element in the list to the upper int(Ceiling) first.\n    Examples:\n    For lst = [1,2,3] the output should be 14\n    For lst = [1,4,9] the output should be 98\n    For lst = [1,3,5,7] the output should be 84\n    For lst = [1.4,4.2,0] the output should be 29\n    For lst = [-2.4,1,1] the output should be 6\n    \"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    for num in lst:\n        ceiling_value = math.ceil(num)\n        total += ceiling_value ** 2\n    return total\n```"}
