{"task_id": "HumanEval/134", "solution": "```python\ndef check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(txt):\n    '''\n    Create a function that returns True if the last character\n    of a given string is an alphabetical character and is not\n    a part of a word, and False otherwise.\n    Note: \"word\" is a group of characters separated by space.\n\n    Examples:\n    check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(\"apple pie\") \u279e False\n    check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(\"apple pi e\") \u279e True\n    check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(\"apple pi e \") \u279e False\n    check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(\"\") \u279e False\n    '''\n    # Handle empty string or whitespace-only string\n    if not txt or txt.isspace():\n        return False\n\n    # Remove trailing spaces to find the actual last character\n    stripped = txt.rstrip()\n\n    # If after stripping there's nothing left, return False\n    if not stripped:\n        return False\n\n    # Get the last character\n    last_char = stripped[-1]\n\n    # Check if it's alphabetical\n    if not last_char.isalpha():\n        return False\n\n    # Check if it's standalone (not part of a word)\n    # A character is standalone if it's preceded by a space\n    # We need to check in the original string (before stripping)\n    # to see if there was a space before this character\n    last_char_pos = len(stripped) - 1\n\n    # If it's the first character, it's standalone\n    if last_char_pos == 0:\n        return True\n\n    # Check if preceded by space in the stripped string\n    return stripped[last_char_pos - 1] == ' '\n```"}
